{"id": "sdqa0152", "pool": "sdqa", "arm": "balanced", "tier": "balanced", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3024153341, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "f4bb2b6e6cb68bd1527d1ee0e21321345a1bb395842da8101a511dc9dc7ef77e", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.7972545623779297, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 5, "query": "Why is heavy metal called heavy metal?", "input_path": "/data/sdqa_audio/sdqa0152.wav", "input_sha256": "1953709908b14c35f9515ae55e13bec6f493cad51d8d9ef75e63046cdbeb023e", "input_samples": 44727, "input_s": 2.795, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.394, "act": 0.9987, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Well, the term \"heavy metal\" in music is actually not related to the weight of the instruments or sounds. It comes from a completely different context. The phrase originally referred to toxic heavy metals like lead and mercury, which can be harmful to health. In the 196 0s, some rock bands started using the term as a play on words because their music was seen as intense or dark, much like how those chemicals are intense in effect. So it's more about the sound being powerful and aggressive than anything physical ly heavy.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 29, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/sdqa/audio/sdqa0152_balanced_local.wav", "sha256": "db93ab5b29797471c41c8ed0461a75d89e2d98ab2ef26f4ae2ce39061a527c03", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 29.28}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.056, 1.101, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.057, 2.102, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 2.795, 2.853, 2.898, 1, 0, 0], [3, 0, 3.795, 3.849, 4.389, 0, 